Themes, fonts and custom colours
================================

Upstream already has the machinery — app.css defines eleven colour variables on :root and app.js has
applyTheme() switching a data-theme attribute, with a Light/Dark/System picker. It just only ever
defines two looks. So this adds looks rather than a new mechanism:

- **matrix** — green on near-black, for screens that should look like a terminal
- **amber** — the same shape at a warm hue
- **ice** — the same shape cold
- **custom** — inherits the dark base, with --bg / --text / --accent set inline on :root from colour
  pickers, so a custom look layers over a theme instead of replacing it

The font family and text size were hard-coded on :root; both are variables now (--ui-font,
--ui-font-size) with pickers, applied as inline properties so they also layer over any theme.

Nothing here touches the Rust side: it is app.css and app.js, which the package already ships.

Verified in a real browser, not by reading the diff:
  dark   #191c20 / #edf0f2 / #8bd5bd
  matrix #05080a / #7dffa8 / #00ff88
  amber  #0b0803 / #ffcf8b / #ffb020
  ice    #060a0f / #cfe9ff / #4fc3f7
  custom inherits, and an inline --bg override lands; setting --ui-font-size: 18px puts the document
  body at 18px.

